@meta
id: 07-09
title: Move by sentence, not by word
chapter: 07
type: lesson
skills: paragraph-motion, goto-line
par: 140
minutes: 8
vscode: click between two sentences and hope you got the space right
open: refunds.md

@brief
Three sentences to insert into this 100 line reference page. Each goes
between two existing sentences, and the sentence already there does not
change.

  1. The paragraph starting on line 24, after the second sentence:

         The window is a configuration value.

  2. The paragraph starting on line 33, after the third sentence:

         Keys are opaque to the provider.

  3. The last paragraph of the file, immediately before its last sentence:

         Signature failures are counted.

Mind the spacing: each inserted sentence needs one space after its full
stop, exactly like its neighbours, and no other whitespace changes.

Words are too small a unit here and paragraphs are too big. Prose has a unit
in between and so does vim.

@keys
| `)` | forward to the start of the next sentence |
| `(` | back to the start of this sentence |
| `2)` | forward two sentences |
| `$` | end of the line |
| `24G` | line 24 |

@hints
A sentence ends at a full stop, a question mark or an exclamation mark.
Vim knows that, and it has a pair of motions for it that sit next to the
paragraph pair on the keyboard.
--
`)` goes to the start of the next sentence and `(` goes back to the start of
the current one. Both take counts, so `2)` is two sentences forward. Landing
on the start of a sentence and pressing `i` puts your text in front of it.
--
For the third one, `G` puts you in the last sentence of the file and `(`
snaps you back to where that sentence begins. That is the trick worth
having: you do not navigate to the start of a sentence, you land anywhere
inside it and then say `(`.

@solution
    24G 2) i The window is a configuration value.  <Esc>
    33G 3) i Keys are opaque to the provider.  <Esc>
    G $ ( i Signature failures are counted.  <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

About a hundred and twenty keystrokes and a hundred of them are the three
sentences.

Sentence motions are the reason vim is usable for prose, and they are badly
under-used by people who only edit code. A paragraph of documentation is
five or six sentences, so `)` and `(` cut it into pieces you can actually
aim at, in the same way `w` cuts a line into words.

What vim counts as a sentence is worth knowing exactly, because it will
occasionally surprise you: a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark,
optionally followed by closing brackets or quotes, then a space or the end
of the line. That is why `2.7.0` in the middle of a line does not end a
sentence but a version number at the end of one does. It is a simple rule
rather than a clever one, and simple rules are the ones you can predict
under pressure.

The third edit is the pattern to remember. Getting to the last sentence of a
document by moving forwards means counting sentences. Getting there by `G`
and then `(` means not counting anything.

@start refunds.md
# Refunds

This page describes how the orders service issues refunds and what the
payment provider guarantees in return. It is written for engineers working
on the service itself rather than for integrators. Everything here applies
to the v2 payments endpoint. The v1 endpoint was removed in 2.7.0.

## Creating a refund

A refund is created against an order, never against a customer. The order
must be in a terminal state, which in practice means `COMPLETED` or
`CANCELLED`. A refund against a `PENDING` order is rejected with
`ORDER_NOT_FOUND`, which is misleading and is tracked in ORD-391. The
provider is called once the local record has been written. If the provider
call fails the local record is marked `FAILED` and left in place for the
reconciliation job to pick up.

Amounts are given in minor units, so 4999 is £49.99. A refund may not
exceed the amount of the order it is issued against. Partial refunds are
allowed and may be repeated until the order total is reached.

## The refund window

Refunds are only issued for orders placed within the last thirty days.
Outside that window the cancellation still succeeds. No money moves and a
cancellation reason of `REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED` is written to the order.
Finance asked for this in 2.7.0 after a run of refunds against orders more
than a year old. The window is measured from `placedAt` and not from the
time the order completed.

## Idempotency

Every call to the provider carries an idempotency key. The key is derived
from the refund id rather than the order id. Two refunds against the same
order therefore get different keys and both go through, which is what you
want for partial refunds. A retry of the same refund reuses the key and the
provider returns the original response. This is the mechanism that failed
in the 2026-08-14 incident.

## Failure modes

A refund can fail in three places and the three are not equivalent. A
validation failure happens before anything is written and is safe to retry
immediately. A provider failure happens after the local record exists and
must be retried by the reconciliation job rather than by the caller. A
timeout is the difficult case, because the provider may or may not have
accepted the refund. Timeouts are always resolved by reconciliation and
never by retrying in the request path.

## Reconciliation

A job runs nightly and compares the local refund records against the
provider ledger for the previous seven days. Anything present on one side
and not the other is written to the reconciliation report. The report is
delivered to finance at 06:00. Discrepancies older than seven days are not
detected by this job at all.

## Currencies

A refund is always issued in the currency of the order. The provider will
not convert, and an attempt to refund in another currency is rejected
before it leaves the service. Orders created before 2.12.0 have no currency
stored and are treated as sterling.

## The admin tool

Support can issue a refund from the admin tool without going through the
API. Those refunds carry a source of `ADMIN` and a note naming the agent.
They are otherwise identical and go through the same provider call. The
reconciliation job does not distinguish between them.

## Metrics

Three counters matter. `refund_issued_total` counts successful refunds by
currency. `refund_failed_total` counts failures by reason and is the one
that should have been alerting on 2026-08-14. `refund_duration_seconds`
is a histogram of the provider call and is the first place to look when
support reports slowness.

## Testing

The provider is stubbed in unit tests and there is no sandbox call. The
integration suite runs against the provider sandbox and is the only place a
real idempotency key is exercised. Sandbox refunds settle instantly, which
hides every timing bug the production provider will find for you.

## Receipts

A receipt is emailed for every refund that reaches a terminal state. The
email is queued rather than sent inline, so a failure in the mail service
cannot fail the refund. Receipts for partial refunds show the remaining
order balance. A refund that fails never produces a receipt.

## Webhooks

The provider sends a webhook for every state change on a refund. Webhooks
are signed and the signature is checked before the body is parsed. A replay
of a webhook already seen is answered with a 200 and otherwise ignored.
Unknown event types are logged and acknowledged, because the provider
disables endpoints that return errors.

